  "posts": [
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000101",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000001",
      "createdByUserId": 910001,
      "body": "This week's parsha shiur: why does the Torah tell us light was created before the sun and moon? Come Thursday at 8 with your questions. Coffee and the Ramban provided.",
      "ref": "Genesis 1:3",
      "minutesAgo": 35
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000102",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011",
      "createdByUserId": 910001,
      "body": "Rashi asks why the Torah begins with creation rather than the first commandment. His answer is about belonging: the whole world is God's, and the land is given, not seized. A different reading of 'in the beginning' than the one most of us learned as children.",
      "ref": "Rashi on Genesis 1:1:1",
      "minutesAgo": 80
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000103",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012",
      "createdByUserId": 910002,
      "body": "Three manuscripts read this line of the Mishnah differently, and the difference decides whether the evening Shema is tied to the priests eating terumah or simply to nightfall. Small letters, large consequences.",
      "ref": "Mishnah Berakhot 1:1",
      "minutesAgo": 140
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000104",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000003",
      "createdByUserId": 910003,
      "body": "Today's daf: Berakhot 2a. We start where the Talmud starts — with a question about time. 'From when may one recite the Shema in the evening?' Join the circle; the first page is the best place to begin.",
      "ref": "Berakhot 2a",
      "minutesAgo": 190
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000105",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014",
      "createdByUserId": 910004,
      "body": "\"The Lord is my shepherd; I lack nothing.\" Not 'I have everything' — 'I lack nothing.' The verse is about enough, not about more.",
      "ref": "Psalms 23:1",
      "minutesAgo": 260
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000106",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013",
      "createdByUserId": 910003,
      "body": "Hillel's whole Torah on one foot: what is hateful to you, do not do to your fellow. The rest is commentary — go and learn it. I keep coming back to the last three words.",
      "ref": "Shabbat 31a",
      "minutesAgo": 330
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000107",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000004",
      "createdByUserId": 910004,
      "body": "מחר בערב: שיעור בתהילים עם נועה שפירא. נלמד את מזמור כ״ג עם המלבי״ם ונדבר על תפילה כשאין מילים. כולן מוזמנות.",
      "ref": "Psalms 23",
      "minutesAgo": 400
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000108",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011",
      "createdByUserId": 910001,
      "body": "'I will be what I will be.' Moshe asks for a name and receives a promise about the future. Onkelos keeps the Hebrew; the Rambam reads it as necessary existence. Two very different Gods in one verse — or one God seen from two sides?",
      "ref": "Exodus 3:14",
      "minutesAgo": 520
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000109",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015",
      "createdByUserId": 910005,
      "body": "First time learning Pirkei Avot with a chavruta. 'On three things the world stands: Torah, avodah and gemilut chasadim.' My chavruta asked which one I do least. Still thinking about it.",
      "ref": "Pirkei Avot 1:2",
      "minutesAgo": 610
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000110",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012",
      "createdByUserId": 910002,
      "body": "\"Love your fellow as yourself\" — Rabbi Akiva calls it a great principle of the Torah. Ben Azzai says there is a greater one: 'This is the book of the generations of Adam.' Love can be measured against the self; dignity cannot.",
      "ref": "Leviticus 19:18",
      "minutesAgo": 760
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000111",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000002",
      "createdByUserId": 910002,
      "body": "New zman, new chavrutot. Our Elul seder starts Sunday: Masechet Megillah in the morning, Sefer Yonah in the afternoon. Applications open to anyone who wants a quiet place to learn.",
      "minutesAgo": 900
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000112",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014",
      "createdByUserId": 910004,
      "body": "\"Comfort, comfort My people.\" The doubling isn't repetition. Chazal read it as comfort for a double loss, and the haftarah of Nachamu answers the double lament of Eichah. Grief and comfort, measured in the same language.",
      "ref": "Isaiah 40:1",
      "minutesAgo": 1100
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000113",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013",
      "createdByUserId": 910003,
      "body": "Siyum on Masechet Berakhot tonight! Fifty-three days, sixty-four pages, one very tired chavruta. Hadran alach.",
      "ref": "Berakhot 64a",
      "minutesAgo": 1400
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000114",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011",
      "createdByUserId": 910001,
      "body": "Ruth's 'where you go, I will go' is the first conversion in the Tanakh and it is made of a relationship before it is made of a theology. Worth sitting with before Shavuot.",
      "ref": "Ruth 1:16",
      "minutesAgo": 1700
    }
  ],
  "replies": [
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000201",
      "postId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000102",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015",
      "createdByUserId": 910005,
      "body": "I never knew that Rashi was the source of this. Is there a commentator who disagrees?",
      "minutesAgo": 70
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000202",
      "postId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000102",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011",
      "createdByUserId": 910001,
      "body": "The Ramban does, at length — he thinks the opening teaches creation ex nihilo first. Bring it Thursday and we'll read them side by side.",
      "minutesAgo": 60
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000203",
      "postId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000105",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000012",
      "createdByUserId": 910002,
      "body": "Beautiful. The Targum renders it 'I will not lack food' — very concrete. Both readings are true at once.",
      "minutesAgo": 240
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000204",
      "postId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000106",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000015",
      "createdByUserId": 910005,
      "body": "'Go and learn it' is the part I needed to hear this week.",
      "minutesAgo": 300
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000205",
      "postId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000108",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000014",
      "createdByUserId": 910004,
      "body": "Rashi reads it as 'I will be with them in this sorrow as I will be with them in future ones.' A third side!",
      "minutesAgo": 500
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000206",
      "postId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000113",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000011",
      "createdByUserId": 910001,
      "body": "Mazal tov! Yasher koach to the whole circle.",
      "minutesAgo": 1380
    },
    {
      "id": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000207",
      "postId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000109",
      "authorProfileId": "de000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000013",
      "createdByUserId": 910003,
      "body": "Gemilut chasadim for me, every time. It's the one you can't do alone.",
      "minutesAgo": 590
    }
  ],
